WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.220 --> 00:00:06.000 line:84%
MADELINE SCHMIDT: Good
evening, everyone.

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:08.029 line:90%
My name is Madeline Schmidt.

00:00:08.029 --> 00:00:12.200 line:84%
I am a junior rhetoric
major at Hillsdale College.

00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.460 line:84%
And it is my absolute pleasure
to introduce Mr. Roger Kimball

00:00:16.460 --> 00:00:18.260 line:90%
this evening.

00:00:18.260 --> 00:00:21.560 line:84%
Roger Kimball is
editor and publisher

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:25.040 line:84%
of The New Criterion and
president and publisher

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:28.790 line:84%
of Encounter Books,
a contributor

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:33.680 line:84%
to numerous publications,
including the Epic Times,

00:00:33.680 --> 00:00:37.580 line:84%
the Sunday Telegraph,
the American Spectator,

00:00:37.580 --> 00:00:39.590 line:90%
and American Greatness.

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:43.430 line:84%
Mr. Kimball is editor,
co-editor, or author

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:48.590 line:84%
of numerous books, including
Athwart History, Half a Century

00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:53.120 line:84%
of Polemics, Animadversions,
and Illuminations,

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.860 line:84%
A William F. Buckley Jr.
Omnibus, The Long March,

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.860 line:84%
How the Cultural
Revolution of the 1960s

00:01:00.860 --> 00:01:04.459 line:84%
Changed America, Vox
Populi, The Perils

00:01:04.459 --> 00:01:10.010 line:84%
and Promises of Populism, and
most recently, Who Rules?,

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.040 line:84%
Sovereignty,
Nationalism, and the Fate

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:16.190 line:90%
of Freedom in the 21st Century.

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:20.690 line:84%
Will everyone please welcome me
in joining Mr. Roger Kimball.

00:01:20.690 --> 00:01:24.029 line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:30.035 --> 00:01:31.410 line:84%
ROGER KIMBALL:
Thank you, Maddie.

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:33.320 line:84%
I thought you were going to say
something about my fabulous bow

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:33.820 line:90%
tie.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:40.880 line:84%
Well, it's a pleasure
to be back at Hillsdale,

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.270 line:90%
a little oasis of sanity.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.940 line:84%
Well, since this conference
is about the liberal arts

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:58.250 line:84%
and education today, and my
own slice of that pie concerns

00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:03.577 line:84%
freedom and education, I'd
like to begin with a question.

00:02:03.577 --> 00:02:05.160 line:84%
And it's one that
I'd like you to keep

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:09.240 line:90%
in mind as the evening unfolds.

00:02:09.240 --> 00:02:14.810 line:84%
The question is
this, what is liberal

00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:17.240 line:90%
about a liberal education?

00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:23.470 line:84%
Now that question is not as easy
to answer as you might think.

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.520 line:84%
For one thing,
really to answer it,

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:28.000 line:84%
you have to know what
the word liberal means.

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.340 line:84%
Can you think of
any word that has

00:02:30.340 --> 00:02:37.180 line:84%
accumulated more conflicting
meanings than the word liberal?

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:38.920 line:84%
And then there's
the word education,

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:41.680 line:84%
who wants to give
that one a try?

00:02:41.680 --> 00:02:45.440 line:84%
In my experience, the more you
think about those simple words,

00:02:45.440 --> 00:02:47.980 line:84%
the more elusive
are their meanings.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.140 line:84%
In the materials distributed
for this conference,

00:02:53.140 --> 00:02:57.400 line:84%
there is a cheery quotation
from James Madison,

00:02:57.400 --> 00:02:59.290 line:84%
co-author of the
Federalist Papers

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:03.400 line:84%
and a principal author
of the US Constitution.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:08.620 line:84%
Madison says that liberty
and learning belong together.

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:11.890 line:84%
They support each
other, he says,

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:17.280 line:84%
and further, their connection
supports a free society.

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.480 line:84%
In various forms, the nexus
between liberty and learning

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:24.330 line:84%
is a very traditional
idea, one that

00:03:24.330 --> 00:03:28.650 line:84%
has epistemological
and existential as well

00:03:28.650 --> 00:03:32.040 line:90%
as political dimensions.

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:35.850 line:84%
Aristotle begins the
book we call Metaphysics

00:03:35.850 --> 00:03:43.140 line:84%
with the observation that all
men, by nature, desire to know.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:46.830 line:84%
Aristotle thought that
learning freed us,

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:50.550 line:84%
gave us liberty from
the snares of ignorance,

00:03:50.550 --> 00:03:53.660 line:90%
illusion, and mere appearance.

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:59.850 line:84%
Learning, he thought, not only
depends upon, but also begets

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:02.890 line:90%
certain freedoms.

00:04:02.890 --> 00:04:04.590 line:84%
In the Gospel,
according to St. John,

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:07.480 line:84%
Jesus says that you
shall know the truth,

00:04:07.480 --> 00:04:11.300 line:84%
and the truth
shall set you free.

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:15.680 line:84%
In the Gospel, the relevant
freedom is freedom from sin.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.630 line:84%
But that point can
be generalized,

00:04:17.630 --> 00:04:22.340 line:84%
which is why various schools
and colleges have adopted

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:25.350 line:90%
the saying as their motto.

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:29.750 line:84%
And it's perhaps worth
noting that the CIA liked

00:04:29.750 --> 00:04:31.790 line:84%
the quotation so
much that they had

00:04:31.790 --> 00:04:35.810 line:84%
it etched into a wall
of their headquarters.

00:04:35.810 --> 00:04:39.020 line:84%
It's amusing to speculate
what the author of that saying

00:04:39.020 --> 00:04:42.545 line:84%
would make of this spooky
application of his words.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:49.040 line:84%
The Greek word that the
evangelist John uses for truth

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:50.950 line:90%
is aletheia--

00:04:50.950 --> 00:04:51.590 line:90%
aletheia.

00:04:51.590 --> 00:04:54.770 line:84%
Interestingly, it
has a negative form--

00:04:54.770 --> 00:04:56.960 line:90%
a, letheia.

00:04:56.960 --> 00:05:01.910 line:84%
Etymologically, it
means not forgetful.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:05.000 line:84%
In Greek mythology,
the River Lethe

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:06.870 line:90%
is a fount of forgetfulness.

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:13.850 line:84%
Truth-- aletheia-- is
the antidote to lethe.

00:05:13.850 --> 00:05:17.420 line:84%
Memory, in other
words, is deeply

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:20.405 line:84%
implicated in the
career of truth.

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:24.800 line:84%
So let's come back
to the question,

00:05:24.800 --> 00:05:30.320 line:84%
what is liberal about a
liberal arts education?

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:34.900 line:84%
As Professor [? Copps ?]
reminded us this afternoon

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:36.820 line:84%
in his excellent
talk, the word liberal

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:41.980 line:84%
comes from the Latin word
liber, which means free.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:45.160 line:84%
The original reason that
a liberal arts education

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:49.750 line:84%
was so prized was because
it promised to free students

00:05:49.750 --> 00:05:54.830 line:84%
from the rust of prejudice
and presentism, the belief

00:05:54.830 --> 00:05:58.210 line:84%
that the present moment
is the summit of wisdom

00:05:58.210 --> 00:06:01.870 line:84%
and enlightenment, and that,
accordingly, the past is

00:06:01.870 --> 00:06:05.920 line:84%
nothing more than a graveyard
of discredited and mostly

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:09.400 line:90%
malignant ideas.

00:06:09.400 --> 00:06:16.830 line:84%
Is liberal education still
liberal in that sense?

00:06:16.830 --> 00:06:19.050 line:84%
I'd wager that
everyone in this room

00:06:19.050 --> 00:06:22.170 line:84%
knows George
Santayana's observation

00:06:22.170 --> 00:06:28.610 line:84%
that he who is ignorant of the
past is destined to repeat it.

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:32.010 line:84%
That is one reason we put
such stock by education.

00:06:32.010 --> 00:06:34.430 line:84%
We believe that,
when successful,

00:06:34.430 --> 00:06:39.350 line:84%
education can help rescue us
from making the same mistakes

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:43.270 line:84%
that earlier
generations had made.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:45.820 line:84%
When I ponder the
recent itinerary

00:06:45.820 --> 00:06:48.010 line:90%
of education in this country--

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:51.580 line:84%
and not just college education,
but the whole shebang--

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:53.980 line:84%
I often think of that
old advertisement

00:06:53.980 --> 00:06:56.650 line:84%
for a brand of
cigarettes designed

00:06:56.650 --> 00:07:00.010 line:90%
to appeal especially to women.

00:07:00.010 --> 00:07:03.160 line:90%
You've come a long way, baby.

00:07:03.160 --> 00:07:05.260 line:90%
How right they were.

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:12.130 line:84%
But a distance traveled is not
necessarily a progress logged.

00:07:12.130 --> 00:07:16.840 line:84%
It was not so long ago that
Cardinal Newman's enumeration

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:19.270 line:84%
of the goals of a
liberal education

00:07:19.270 --> 00:07:23.050 line:84%
could have been taken as a
motto by the American academic

00:07:23.050 --> 00:07:24.790 line:90%
establishment.

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:28.210 line:84%
Newman said that the chief
objects of a university

00:07:28.210 --> 00:07:30.520 line:90%
education were-- and I quote--

00:07:30.520 --> 00:07:34.050 line:84%
"a cultivated intellect,
a delicate taste,

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:37.780 line:84%
a candid, equitable,
dispassionate mind,

00:07:37.780 --> 00:07:44.510 line:84%
a noble and courteous bearing
in the conduct of life."

00:07:44.510 --> 00:07:49.110 line:84%
That educational ambition was
quite normal in the 1850s,

00:07:49.110 --> 00:07:49.610 line:90%
but today?

00:07:52.280 --> 00:07:57.170 line:84%
Or consider the observation made
by the philosopher John Searle,

00:07:57.170 --> 00:08:00.710 line:84%
that, quote, "the idea that the
curriculum should be converted

00:08:00.710 --> 00:08:05.030 line:84%
to any partisan
purposes is a perversion

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:07.010 line:90%
of the ideal of a university.

00:08:09.710 --> 00:08:12.080 line:84%
The attempt to
convert the curriculum

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:17.060 line:84%
into an instrument of social
transformation," he said,

00:08:17.060 --> 00:08:19.850 line:84%
"whether it be leftist,
rightist, centrist,

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:26.330 line:84%
or whatever, is the very
opposite of higher education."

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:30.050 line:84%
Until the day before
yesterday, Searle's warning

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:33.230 line:90%
was regarded as common sense.

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:38.780 line:84%
Now it is uncommon and
highly provocative wisdom.

00:08:38.780 --> 00:08:42.830 line:84%
How easy it is to
forget the journey that

00:08:42.830 --> 00:08:46.510 line:90%
led us into this situation.

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:49.270 line:84%
Now I'm not suggesting
that in the past

00:08:49.270 --> 00:08:51.640 line:84%
our educational
institutions always

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:56.140 line:84%
lived up perfectly to the
ideal that Newman enunciated,

00:08:56.140 --> 00:08:59.890 line:84%
or that they always avoided
the perversion against which

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:04.360 line:84%
Professor Searle warned,
but they aspired to.

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:07.790 line:84%
Indeed, until at least
the middle 1960s,

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:12.340 line:84%
there was robust agreement
about the intellectual and moral

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:15.010 line:84%
goals of a liberal
arts education,

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:20.270 line:84%
even if those goals seemed
difficult to achieve.

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:22.660 line:84%
There was, for example,
a shared commitment

00:09:22.660 --> 00:09:27.160 line:84%
to the ideal of disinterested
scholarship devoted

00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:30.520 line:84%
to the preservation and
transmission of knowledge,

00:09:30.520 --> 00:09:36.040 line:84%
which meant the preservation and
transmission of a civilization

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:42.780 line:84%
pursued in a community free
from ideological intimidation.

00:09:42.780 --> 00:09:45.570 line:84%
If we inevitably fell
short of the ideal,

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:49.290 line:84%
the ideal nevertheless
continued to command respect

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:54.520 line:84%
and to exert a
guiding influence.

00:09:54.520 --> 00:09:58.090 line:84%
The truth is that despite
widespread concern

00:09:58.090 --> 00:10:00.250 line:84%
about the fate of
higher education

00:10:00.250 --> 00:10:02.740 line:84%
and despite many
and various efforts

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:08.350 line:84%
to call attention and remedy it,
the reality is, in many ways,

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:14.770 line:84%
far graver today than it
was in the 1970s and 1980s

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:18.730 line:84%
when exotic phenomena,
such as Afrocentrism,

00:10:18.730 --> 00:10:24.010 line:84%
post-colonial studies, queer
theory, critical legal studies,

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:28.150 line:84%
and the attack on science by
anti-humanistic humanists,

00:10:28.150 --> 00:10:31.600 line:84%
was just beginning
to gather steam.

00:10:31.600 --> 00:10:34.090 line:84%
And despite the rise
of alternative voices

00:10:34.090 --> 00:10:35.230 line:90%
here and there--

00:10:35.230 --> 00:10:39.760 line:84%
and since I'm at Hillsdale,
let me stress the word here--

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:43.390 line:84%
those dominating the
discussion at most institutions

00:10:43.390 --> 00:10:46.690 line:84%
are committed to discrediting
the traditional humanistic

00:10:46.690 --> 00:10:50.830 line:84%
ideals of liberal education
by injecting politics

00:10:50.830 --> 00:10:56.140 line:84%
into the heart of the
educational enterprise.

00:10:56.140 --> 00:11:00.400 line:84%
Consider the phenomenon-- the
phenomena of multiculturalism

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:02.500 line:90%
and political correctness.

00:11:02.500 --> 00:11:05.560 line:84%
And I always think of putting
scare quotes around the word

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:07.810 line:84%
multiculturalism,
because, really, it

00:11:07.810 --> 00:11:11.380 line:84%
is a form of monocultural
animus directed

00:11:11.380 --> 00:11:13.960 line:90%
against the dominant culture.

00:11:13.960 --> 00:11:16.570 line:84%
The multiculturalists
claim to be fostering

00:11:16.570 --> 00:11:20.350 line:84%
a progressive cultural
cosmopolitanism

00:11:20.350 --> 00:11:24.580 line:84%
distinguished by superior
sensitivity to the downtrodden

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:27.500 line:90%
and dispossessed.

00:11:27.500 --> 00:11:30.680 line:84%
In fact, they
encourage an orgy of

00:11:30.680 --> 00:11:34.430 line:84%
self-flagellating
liberal guilt as impotent

00:11:34.430 --> 00:11:36.980 line:90%
as it is insatiable.

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:41.450 line:84%
Hence, the woke sensitivity
of the multiculturalist

00:11:41.450 --> 00:11:44.180 line:84%
is an index not of
moral refinement

00:11:44.180 --> 00:11:47.890 line:90%
but of moral vacuousness.

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:51.560 line:84%
Multiculturalism is a
paralyzing intoxicant.

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:57.710 line:84%
Its thrill centers around the
emotion of superior virtue.

00:11:57.710 --> 00:12:00.760 line:84%
Its hangover subsists
on a diet of ignorance

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:04.880 line:90%
and blighted good intentions.

00:12:04.880 --> 00:12:08.090 line:84%
The crucial thing to understand
about multiculturalism

00:12:08.090 --> 00:12:11.990 line:84%
is that not withstanding its
emancipationist rhetoric,

00:12:11.990 --> 00:12:15.980 line:84%
it is not about recognizing
the general cultural diversity

00:12:15.980 --> 00:12:18.620 line:90%
or encouraging pluralism.

00:12:18.620 --> 00:12:21.410 line:84%
It is about undermining
the priority

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:25.490 line:84%
of Western liberal values
in our educational system

00:12:25.490 --> 00:12:29.180 line:84%
and, indeed, in our
society at large.

00:12:29.180 --> 00:12:32.510 line:84%
In essence, as the political
scientist Samuel Huntington

00:12:32.510 --> 00:12:35.780 line:84%
pointed out,
multiculturalism is, quote,

00:12:35.780 --> 00:12:39.420 line:90%
"anti-European civilization.

00:12:39.420 --> 00:12:42.210 line:84%
It is basically,"
said Huntington,

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:46.450 line:90%
"an anti-Western ideology."

00:12:46.450 --> 00:12:50.190 line:84%
Now the most ironical aspect
of this whole spectacle

00:12:50.190 --> 00:12:52.620 line:84%
is that what appears
to its adherents

00:12:52.620 --> 00:12:57.690 line:84%
as bravely anti-Western is,
in fact, a part of the West's

00:12:57.690 --> 00:13:01.320 line:90%
long tradition of self-scrutiny.

00:13:01.320 --> 00:13:06.090 line:84%
Indeed, criticism of the West
has been a prominent ingredient

00:13:06.090 --> 00:13:08.610 line:84%
in the West's
self-understanding,

00:13:08.610 --> 00:13:12.480 line:84%
at least since Socrates
invited his fellow Athenians

00:13:12.480 --> 00:13:16.890 line:84%
to debate with him about
the nature of the good life.

00:13:16.890 --> 00:13:20.430 line:84%
No civilization in history
has been as consistently

00:13:20.430 --> 00:13:23.340 line:90%
self-critical as the West.

00:13:23.340 --> 00:13:25.560 line:84%
In attacking Western
civilization,

00:13:25.560 --> 00:13:29.850 line:84%
the multiculturalist is also
attacking the liberating spirit

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:34.800 line:84%
of self-scrutiny, replacing
it with fashionable dogmas

00:13:34.800 --> 00:13:35.880 line:90%
of indoctrination.

00:13:38.870 --> 00:13:42.890 line:84%
Anti-Americanism occupies
such a prominent place

00:13:42.890 --> 00:13:45.380 line:84%
on the agenda of
the culture wars,

00:13:45.380 --> 00:13:48.170 line:84%
precisely because the
traditional values

00:13:48.170 --> 00:13:52.070 line:84%
of American identity
articulated by the founders

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:56.030 line:84%
and grounded in a commitment
to individual liberty,

00:13:56.030 --> 00:14:01.220 line:84%
limited government, and public
virtue are deeply at odds

00:14:01.220 --> 00:14:04.220 line:84%
with the radical,
decivilizing tenants

00:14:04.220 --> 00:14:06.470 line:84%
of the multiculturalist
enterprise

00:14:06.470 --> 00:14:09.290 line:90%
of political correctness.

00:14:09.290 --> 00:14:13.010 line:84%
A profound ignorance of
the milestones of American,

00:14:13.010 --> 00:14:17.580 line:84%
or indeed of any other culture,
is one predictable result.

00:14:17.580 --> 00:14:19.910 line:84%
The statistics have
become proverbial.

00:14:19.910 --> 00:14:21.920 line:84%
Huntington quotes
one poll showing

00:14:21.920 --> 00:14:25.370 line:84%
that while 90% of
Ivy League students

00:14:25.370 --> 00:14:28.910 line:84%
could identify Rosa
Parks, only 25%

00:14:28.910 --> 00:14:32.150 line:84%
could identify the author
of the words "government

00:14:32.150 --> 00:14:35.840 line:84%
of the people, by the
people, for the people."

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:42.980 line:84%
Another survey revealed that 40%
of seniors at 55 top colleges

00:14:42.980 --> 00:14:45.290 line:84%
could not say within
half a century

00:14:45.290 --> 00:14:49.460 line:90%
when the Civil War was fought.

00:14:49.460 --> 00:14:55.400 line:84%
The erosion of shared memory is
such a potent tool of tyranny

00:14:55.400 --> 00:14:58.460 line:84%
because it dissolves those
links with the past that

00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:01.580 line:90%
give substance to liberty.

00:15:01.580 --> 00:15:03.500 line:84%
The founding father,
James Wilson,

00:15:03.500 --> 00:15:05.810 line:84%
was right when he
observed that, quote,

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:10.250 line:84%
"law and liberty cannot
rationally become the object

00:15:10.250 --> 00:15:14.240 line:84%
of our love, unless they
first become the objects

00:15:14.240 --> 00:15:16.670 line:90%
of our knowledge.

00:15:16.670 --> 00:15:23.010 line:84%
We must know the past in order
to profit from its lessons."

00:15:23.010 --> 00:15:26.790 line:84%
It almost goes without saying
that the tenured, or soon

00:15:26.790 --> 00:15:30.120 line:84%
to be tenured, radicals
now controlling nearly all

00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:32.490 line:84%
of the most prestigious
humanities departments

00:15:32.490 --> 00:15:36.780 line:84%
in this country reply that
their critics have overstated

00:15:36.780 --> 00:15:37.830 line:90%
the case.

00:15:37.830 --> 00:15:41.160 line:84%
Really, they say,
there is nothing amiss.

00:15:41.160 --> 00:15:45.720 line:84%
Nothing has happened that need
concern parents, trustees,

00:15:45.720 --> 00:15:51.090 line:84%
alumni, government, or other
private funding sources.

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:54.120 line:84%
On the issue of enforcing
politically correct behavior

00:15:54.120 --> 00:15:57.960 line:84%
on campus, for example, they
will assure you that the whole

00:15:57.960 --> 00:16:02.110 line:84%
thing has been overblown by
conservative journalists who do

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:05.610 line:84%
not sufficiently admire
multiculturalism and cannot

00:16:05.610 --> 00:16:09.270 line:84%
appreciate that the free
exchange of ideas must

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:13.830 line:84%
sometimes be curtailed for the
higher virtue of protecting

00:16:13.830 --> 00:16:17.910 line:84%
the feelings of
designated victim groups.

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:21.120 line:84%
And the curriculum, they will
say, has not been politicized.

00:16:21.120 --> 00:16:23.970 line:84%
It has been merely
democratized, opened up

00:16:23.970 --> 00:16:27.090 line:84%
to reflect the differing
needs and standards

00:16:27.090 --> 00:16:31.440 line:84%
of groups and ideas hitherto
insufficiently represented

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:32.250 line:90%
in the Academy.

00:16:35.230 --> 00:16:40.450 line:84%
The aim of such objections is
not to enlighten or persuade,

00:16:40.450 --> 00:16:44.530 line:84%
but to intimidate and
to pre-empt criticism.

00:16:44.530 --> 00:16:47.170 line:84%
The truth is that what
we are facing today

00:16:47.170 --> 00:16:50.320 line:84%
is nothing less
than the destruction

00:16:50.320 --> 00:16:52.720 line:84%
of the fundamental
premises that underlie

00:16:52.720 --> 00:16:56.050 line:84%
our conception both
of a liberal education

00:16:56.050 --> 00:17:00.620 line:84%
and of a liberal
democratic polity.

00:17:00.620 --> 00:17:04.790 line:84%
Respect for rationality and
the rights of the individual,

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:08.450 line:84%
a commitment to the ideals
of disinterested criticism

00:17:08.450 --> 00:17:13.310 line:84%
and colorblind justice,
advancement according to merit,

00:17:13.310 --> 00:17:17.690 line:84%
not according to sex,
race, or ethnic origin,

00:17:17.690 --> 00:17:20.450 line:84%
these quintessentially
Western ideas

00:17:20.450 --> 00:17:25.609 line:84%
are bedrocks of our political as
well as our educational system.

00:17:25.609 --> 00:17:28.040 line:84%
And they are precisely
the ideas that

00:17:28.040 --> 00:17:31.760 line:84%
are now under attack by
progressive academics

00:17:31.760 --> 00:17:36.020 line:84%
intoxicated by the coercive
possibilities generated

00:17:36.020 --> 00:17:38.750 line:84%
by their
self-infatuating embrace

00:17:38.750 --> 00:17:42.510 line:90%
of political correctness.

00:17:42.510 --> 00:17:45.180 line:84%
Political correctness
also fosters an atmosphere

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:49.200 line:84%
of intimidation and
encourages a slavish,

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:52.140 line:84%
moral, and intellectual
conformity,

00:17:52.140 --> 00:17:55.860 line:84%
attacking the very basis for
the free exchange of ideas.

00:17:55.860 --> 00:18:01.080 line:84%
Even worse, it encourages a kind
of intellectual sentimentality

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:04.650 line:84%
that makes it difficult to
acknowledge certain unpalatable

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:07.440 line:84%
realities, the
reality, for example,

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:10.860 line:84%
that not all cultures, or,
indeed, all individuals,

00:18:10.860 --> 00:18:14.790 line:84%
are equal in terms of
potential or accomplishment.

00:18:14.790 --> 00:18:18.930 line:84%
It insinuates that
dreadful lie in the soul

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:23.360 line:84%
that Socrates warned
about in the Republic.

00:18:23.360 --> 00:18:27.530 line:84%
Politics, the late journalist
Andrew Breitbart once observed,

00:18:27.530 --> 00:18:30.830 line:90%
is downstream from culture.

00:18:30.830 --> 00:18:35.810 line:84%
In the United States, a
primary engine of culture

00:18:35.810 --> 00:18:38.450 line:84%
is the educational
establishment.

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:41.150 line:84%
Since the late
1960s, it has been

00:18:41.150 --> 00:18:46.550 line:84%
anything but an ivory tower, a
quiet, semi-cloistered redoubt

00:18:46.550 --> 00:18:52.130 line:84%
deliberately subsisting at one
remove from partisan passions.

00:18:52.130 --> 00:18:55.430 line:84%
On the contrary, the educational
establishment in this country--

00:18:55.430 --> 00:18:58.790 line:84%
and this goes for primary and
secondary education as well

00:18:58.790 --> 00:19:00.200 line:90%
as for colleges--

00:19:00.200 --> 00:19:02.750 line:84%
has incorporated those
partisan passions

00:19:02.750 --> 00:19:05.540 line:90%
as part of its raison d'etre.

00:19:05.540 --> 00:19:10.490 line:84%
The chief question is this,
should our institutions

00:19:10.490 --> 00:19:13.520 line:84%
of higher education
be devoted primarily

00:19:13.520 --> 00:19:16.490 line:84%
to education of
citizens, or should they

00:19:16.490 --> 00:19:22.940 line:84%
be laboratories for social
and political experimentation?

00:19:22.940 --> 00:19:25.220 line:84%
Traditionally, a
liberal arts education

00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:29.970 line:84%
involved both character
formation and learning.

00:19:29.970 --> 00:19:33.290 line:84%
The goal was to produce
men and women who,

00:19:33.290 --> 00:19:38.030 line:84%
as Allan Bloom memorably put
it, had reflected thoughtfully

00:19:38.030 --> 00:19:41.930 line:84%
on the question of what is
man in relation to his highest

00:19:41.930 --> 00:19:47.730 line:84%
aspirations as opposed to
his low and common needs.

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:50.820 line:84%
In recent decades, however,
colleges and universities

00:19:50.820 --> 00:19:54.720 line:84%
have more and more been home to
what the literary critic Lionel

00:19:54.720 --> 00:19:59.640 line:84%
Trilling called the adversary
culture of intellectuals.

00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:06.280 line:84%
The goal is rejection and
repudiation, not reflection.

00:20:06.280 --> 00:20:08.310 line:84%
Now the key issue,
I hasten to add,

00:20:08.310 --> 00:20:14.760 line:84%
is not partisan politics per
se, but rather the subordinating

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:17.820 line:84%
of intellectual life
to non-intellectual,

00:20:17.820 --> 00:20:20.610 line:84%
that is to say
political imperatives.

00:20:20.610 --> 00:20:23.940 line:84%
Indeed, it is this
failure, a failure

00:20:23.940 --> 00:20:27.030 line:84%
to check the colonization
of intellectual life

00:20:27.030 --> 00:20:32.250 line:84%
by politics, that stands behind
and fuels the degradation

00:20:32.250 --> 00:20:34.450 line:90%
of liberal education.

00:20:34.450 --> 00:20:39.270 line:84%
The issue is less the
presence of bad politics

00:20:39.270 --> 00:20:43.140 line:84%
than the absence of non-politics
in the intellectual life

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:44.160 line:90%
of the university.

00:20:46.820 --> 00:20:49.760 line:84%
Now what has happened to the
educational establishment

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:53.720 line:84%
cannot be understood from
its cultural context,

00:20:53.720 --> 00:20:57.230 line:84%
that long march through the
institutions that the Italian

00:20:57.230 --> 00:21:00.860 line:84%
Marxist Antonio
Gramsci recommended,

00:21:00.860 --> 00:21:04.790 line:84%
and whose American liniments I
chronicled in my book The Long

00:21:04.790 --> 00:21:09.230 line:84%
March, How the Cultural
Revolution of the 1960s Changed

00:21:09.230 --> 00:21:11.180 line:90%
America.

00:21:11.180 --> 00:21:14.300 line:84%
The Age of Aquarius, I wrote
in that book's introduction,

00:21:14.300 --> 00:21:16.910 line:84%
did not end when the
last electric guitar

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:18.710 line:90%
was unplugged at Woodstock.

00:21:18.710 --> 00:21:23.810 line:84%
It lives on in our values, and
our habits, and our tastes,

00:21:23.810 --> 00:21:25.880 line:90%
pleasures, and aspirations.

00:21:25.880 --> 00:21:29.450 line:84%
It lives on especially in
our emotional and cultural

00:21:29.450 --> 00:21:33.530 line:84%
institutions and in the degraded
pop culture that permeates

00:21:33.530 --> 00:21:35.840 line:90%
our lives like a corrosive fog.

00:21:39.190 --> 00:21:41.890 line:84%
We have been seeing the
results of this dissemination

00:21:41.890 --> 00:21:46.540 line:84%
everywhere-- in the media, in
the corporate boardrooms where

00:21:46.540 --> 00:21:49.390 line:84%
resolutions deploring
systemic racism

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:54.100 line:84%
are touted by quivering,
virtue-signaling bureaucrats,

00:21:54.100 --> 00:21:56.650 line:84%
throughout the federal
government where workshops

00:21:56.650 --> 00:22:00.340 line:84%
on critical race
theory catechize

00:22:00.340 --> 00:22:03.430 line:84%
government workers about
the evils of whiteness

00:22:03.430 --> 00:22:06.490 line:84%
and the glories
of trans culture,

00:22:06.490 --> 00:22:10.000 line:84%
and just this past summer
on our city streets

00:22:10.000 --> 00:22:15.610 line:84%
where Antifa and the trained
Marxists of Black Lives Matter

00:22:15.610 --> 00:22:19.180 line:84%
rampaged under the protection
of the First Amendment

00:22:19.180 --> 00:22:22.780 line:84%
while endeavoring to destroy
the political culture that

00:22:22.780 --> 00:22:27.260 line:90%
underwrites such protections.

00:22:27.260 --> 00:22:30.700 line:84%
It is a great irony that the
free speech movement, which

00:22:30.700 --> 00:22:35.660 line:84%
began in 1964 in the tumult of
Berkeley, has over the years

00:22:35.660 --> 00:22:39.830 line:84%
mutated into something
close to the opposite,

00:22:39.830 --> 00:22:42.820 line:84%
a kind of anti-free
speech movement

00:22:42.820 --> 00:22:46.000 line:84%
that approaches opinions
with which it disagrees

00:22:46.000 --> 00:22:49.140 line:90%
by attempting to cancel them.

00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:53.060 line:84%
It is not at all uncommon
on our nation's campuses

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:56.880 line:84%
to find academics decrying
academic freedom in the name

00:22:56.880 --> 00:22:59.820 line:90%
of a putatively higher virtue.

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:03.690 line:84%
At many institutions,
liberal education

00:23:03.690 --> 00:23:09.650 line:84%
has mutated into
intolerant indoctrination.

00:23:09.650 --> 00:23:13.490 line:84%
A similar process of mutation
or reversal has been at work

00:23:13.490 --> 00:23:17.180 line:84%
throughout the so-called
liberal institutions of Western

00:23:17.180 --> 00:23:18.840 line:90%
society--

00:23:18.840 --> 00:23:22.610 line:84%
in the corporate boardrooms
no less than in the media,

00:23:22.610 --> 00:23:25.610 line:84%
government bureaucracies, and
such cultural institutions

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:27.230 line:90%
as museums.

00:23:27.230 --> 00:23:31.730 line:84%
Indeed, the metastasis
of illiberal liberalism

00:23:31.730 --> 00:23:35.450 line:84%
beyond the protected
purlieus of academia

00:23:35.450 --> 00:23:40.040 line:84%
is one of the signal cultural
deformations of our time.

00:23:40.040 --> 00:23:44.420 line:84%
What it tokens is nothing less
than the potential eclipse

00:23:44.420 --> 00:23:47.870 line:84%
of that robust understanding
of liberal society

00:23:47.870 --> 00:23:50.450 line:84%
that has informed the
self-understanding

00:23:50.450 --> 00:23:55.460 line:84%
of the Anglosphere, at least
from the time of Runnymede

00:23:55.460 --> 00:23:58.500 line:90%
and the adoption of Magna Carta.

00:23:58.500 --> 00:24:02.010 line:84%
At its core stand the
ideals of free speech,

00:24:02.010 --> 00:24:06.660 line:84%
religious liberty, and
equality before the law.

00:24:06.660 --> 00:24:09.630 line:84%
The repudiation of such
values and the value

00:24:09.630 --> 00:24:13.290 line:84%
of limited government, which
is a political precondition

00:24:13.290 --> 00:24:15.420 line:84%
for the thriving
of those ideals,

00:24:15.420 --> 00:24:18.330 line:84%
has underwritten the
redefinition of liberalism

00:24:18.330 --> 00:24:22.530 line:84%
from the championship
of freedom to illiberal

00:24:22.530 --> 00:24:24.810 line:90%
politically correct conformity.

00:24:27.930 --> 00:24:31.470 line:84%
It's perhaps worth pausing
to remind ourselves

00:24:31.470 --> 00:24:35.730 line:84%
that free speech does
not exist in isolation.

00:24:35.730 --> 00:24:38.040 line:84%
It is part of a
constellation of freedoms

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:41.520 line:84%
that include, for example,
freedom of conscience

00:24:41.520 --> 00:24:43.860 line:84%
and the freedom
implicit in the idea

00:24:43.860 --> 00:24:47.130 line:84%
that all men are
equal before the law.

00:24:47.130 --> 00:24:52.020 line:84%
Those freedoms are, or
were, bedrock principles

00:24:52.020 --> 00:24:54.540 line:90%
in the modern secular West.

00:24:54.540 --> 00:24:57.690 line:84%
But increasingly, they are
up for grabs in a situation

00:24:57.690 --> 00:25:02.550 line:84%
where dissent from orthodoxy
is met not with disagreement

00:25:02.550 --> 00:25:05.790 line:84%
but with efforts at
suppression, scapegoating,

00:25:05.790 --> 00:25:13.570 line:84%
and ostracism, the entire armory
of so-called cancel culture.

00:25:13.570 --> 00:25:15.460 line:84%
It is worth noting,
too, how many

00:25:15.460 --> 00:25:18.850 line:84%
new disciples of
intolerance begin

00:25:18.850 --> 00:25:22.270 line:84%
by proclaiming their
allegiance to freedom only

00:25:22.270 --> 00:25:25.000 line:90%
to redefine it out of existence.

00:25:25.000 --> 00:25:28.270 line:84%
Freedom of speech is one thing,
said one such campaigner,

00:25:28.270 --> 00:25:31.210 line:84%
but usage of your
freedom should not

00:25:31.210 --> 00:25:33.880 line:84%
be to offend others or
advocate hate speech

00:25:33.880 --> 00:25:36.490 line:90%
or provoke people to violence.

00:25:36.490 --> 00:25:38.320 line:90%
Note that little word but.

00:25:38.320 --> 00:25:41.990 line:84%
Freedom of speech
is one thing-- but.

00:25:41.990 --> 00:25:43.700 line:90%
But what?

00:25:43.700 --> 00:25:47.870 line:84%
A common marker in this debate
is the phrase hate speech.

00:25:47.870 --> 00:25:50.610 line:84%
We're supposed to say
yes to free speech

00:25:50.610 --> 00:25:53.210 line:90%
but deplore hate speech.

00:25:53.210 --> 00:25:56.270 line:84%
Speech, as one partisan
of this Milquetoast

00:25:56.270 --> 00:25:58.400 line:84%
version of free
speech put it, that

00:25:58.400 --> 00:26:02.390 line:90%
offends or insults in general.

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:05.780 line:84%
Now of course, the law has long
had statutes against speech

00:26:05.780 --> 00:26:09.320 line:84%
that incites violence or
that imperils public order--

00:26:09.320 --> 00:26:13.940 line:84%
gratuitously crying fire in a
crowded theater, for example.

00:26:13.940 --> 00:26:17.720 line:84%
But the phrase hate speech
is like that other item

00:26:17.720 --> 00:26:22.190 line:84%
in the lexicon of
less leftist redress--

00:26:22.190 --> 00:26:24.350 line:90%
social justice.

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:28.640 line:84%
It is a weapon masquerading
as a moral imperative.

00:26:28.640 --> 00:26:31.490 line:84%
The adjective is
cognitively vacuous

00:26:31.490 --> 00:26:33.500 line:90%
but emotionally charged.

00:26:33.500 --> 00:26:39.120 line:84%
It injects an intoxicating dose
of moral self-righteousness

00:26:39.120 --> 00:26:46.220 line:84%
that clouds the head, even if
it sets the heart aflutter.

00:26:46.220 --> 00:26:48.640 line:84%
Now the large issue
here is one that

00:26:48.640 --> 00:26:51.070 line:84%
has bedeviled liberal
societies ever

00:26:51.070 --> 00:26:54.040 line:84%
since there were
liberal societies.

00:26:54.040 --> 00:26:58.540 line:84%
Namely, that in attempting to
create the maximally tolerant

00:26:58.540 --> 00:27:02.260 line:84%
society, we also
give scope to those

00:27:02.260 --> 00:27:07.780 line:84%
who would prefer to create the
maximally intolerant society.

00:27:07.780 --> 00:27:11.800 line:84%
It's the old Leninist
credo in action.

00:27:11.800 --> 00:27:16.830 line:84%
Demand freedom, toleration, and
diversity when out of power.

00:27:16.830 --> 00:27:20.590 line:84%
Practice suppression,
control, and the elimination

00:27:20.590 --> 00:27:23.650 line:90%
of opponents when in power.

00:27:23.650 --> 00:27:25.540 line:90%
It's a curious phenomenon.

00:27:25.540 --> 00:27:30.780 line:84%
Liberalism implies openness
to other points of view, even,

00:27:30.780 --> 00:27:34.120 line:84%
it would seem, to those
points of view whose success

00:27:34.120 --> 00:27:36.340 line:90%
would destroy liberalism.

00:27:36.340 --> 00:27:39.640 line:84%
Extending tolerance to
those points of view

00:27:39.640 --> 00:27:43.000 line:90%
is a prescription for suicide.

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:46.450 line:84%
But intolerance betrays
the fundamental premise

00:27:46.450 --> 00:27:50.500 line:90%
of liberalism, namely openness.

00:27:50.500 --> 00:27:52.890 line:84%
As Robert Frost once
put it, a liberal

00:27:52.890 --> 00:27:56.305 line:84%
is someone who refuses to take
his own part in an argument.

00:27:59.940 --> 00:28:02.270 line:84%
The escape from this
disease of liberalism

00:28:02.270 --> 00:28:06.260 line:84%
lies in understanding that
tolerance and openness must

00:28:06.260 --> 00:28:10.940 line:84%
be limited by positive values
if they are not to be vacuous.

00:28:10.940 --> 00:28:13.790 line:84%
American democracy,
for example, affords

00:28:13.790 --> 00:28:17.180 line:84%
its citizens great
latitude, but great latitude

00:28:17.180 --> 00:28:23.630 line:84%
is not synonymous with the
proposition that anything goes.

00:28:23.630 --> 00:28:26.550 line:84%
Allan Bloom put it
well, "the fact,"

00:28:26.550 --> 00:28:29.180 line:84%
he wrote in The Closing
of the American Mind,

00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:33.950 line:84%
"that there have been different
opinions about good and bad

00:28:33.950 --> 00:28:36.530 line:84%
in different times
and places in no way

00:28:36.530 --> 00:28:42.470 line:84%
proves that none is true
or superior to others."

00:28:42.470 --> 00:28:45.400 line:84%
Our society, like every
society, is founded

00:28:45.400 --> 00:28:48.580 line:90%
on particular positive values--

00:28:48.580 --> 00:28:51.220 line:84%
the rule of law,
for example, respect

00:28:51.220 --> 00:28:53.740 line:84%
for the individual,
religious freedom,

00:28:53.740 --> 00:28:56.980 line:84%
the separation of
church and state.

00:28:56.980 --> 00:29:00.100 line:84%
Or think of that robust
liberalism expressed

00:29:00.100 --> 00:29:04.840 line:84%
by Sir Charles Napier, the
British commander in India

00:29:04.840 --> 00:29:09.400 line:84%
in the early 19th century,
told that immolating widows

00:29:09.400 --> 00:29:12.370 line:84%
on the funeral pyres
of their husbands

00:29:12.370 --> 00:29:14.980 line:90%
was a cherished local custom.

00:29:14.980 --> 00:29:18.590 line:84%
Napier said, very well,
we also have a custom.

00:29:18.590 --> 00:29:22.330 line:84%
When men burn a woman alive, we
tie a rope around their necks

00:29:22.330 --> 00:29:24.430 line:90%
and we hang them.

00:29:24.430 --> 00:29:26.290 line:90%
Build your funeral pyre.

00:29:26.290 --> 00:29:29.050 line:84%
Beside it, my carpenters
will build a gallows.

00:29:29.050 --> 00:29:32.320 line:84%
You may follow your custom,
then we will follow ours.

00:29:35.220 --> 00:29:38.880 line:84%
The point is that the openness
that liberal society rightly

00:29:38.880 --> 00:29:43.860 line:84%
cherishes is not a vacuous
openness to all points of view.

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:46.800 line:90%
It is not value neutral.

00:29:46.800 --> 00:29:48.840 line:84%
It need not--
indeed, it cannot--

00:29:48.840 --> 00:29:51.180 line:90%
say yes to all comers--

00:29:51.180 --> 00:29:55.620 line:84%
to the Islamofascist, who,
after all, has his point of view

00:29:55.620 --> 00:30:00.210 line:84%
just as much as the
soccer mom, who has hers.

00:30:00.210 --> 00:30:02.550 line:84%
Western democratic
society is rooted

00:30:02.550 --> 00:30:04.800 line:84%
in a particular vision
of what Aristotle

00:30:04.800 --> 00:30:07.410 line:90%
called the good for man.

00:30:07.410 --> 00:30:12.600 line:84%
The question is-- the question
is, do we, as a society, still

00:30:12.600 --> 00:30:17.760 line:84%
have confidence in the
animating values of that vision?

00:30:17.760 --> 00:30:22.300 line:84%
Do we possess the requisite
will to defend them?

00:30:22.300 --> 00:30:25.510 line:84%
Or was the French philosopher
Jean-Francois Revel

00:30:25.510 --> 00:30:29.590 line:84%
right when he said that
"democratic civilization is

00:30:29.590 --> 00:30:34.570 line:84%
the first in history to blame
itself because another power

00:30:34.570 --> 00:30:37.520 line:90%
is trying to destroy it."

00:30:37.520 --> 00:30:40.370 line:84%
The jury is still out
on these questions,

00:30:40.370 --> 00:30:43.160 line:84%
and how they are answered
will determine the future

00:30:43.160 --> 00:30:45.950 line:84%
not only of Western
universities,

00:30:45.950 --> 00:30:51.380 line:84%
but also of that astonishing,
spiritual, political experiment

00:30:51.380 --> 00:30:55.980 line:84%
that is Western
democratic liberalism.

00:30:55.980 --> 00:30:58.320 line:84%
That liberalism is
embodied in the virtues

00:30:58.320 --> 00:31:01.440 line:84%
of disinterestedness
and impartiality,

00:31:01.440 --> 00:31:04.660 line:84%
the unbiased
discernment of truth.

00:31:04.660 --> 00:31:08.460 line:84%
It is symbolized, for example,
by the statue of justice

00:31:08.460 --> 00:31:11.340 line:84%
adorning our
courthouses, blindfolded

00:31:11.340 --> 00:31:16.590 line:84%
and holding a scale to declare
her allegiance to law, not men.

00:31:16.590 --> 00:31:20.460 line:84%
It is implicit in Martin
Luther King Jr.'s observation

00:31:20.460 --> 00:31:24.750 line:84%
that what matters is not
the color of your skin

00:31:24.750 --> 00:31:28.200 line:84%
but the content
of your character.

00:31:28.200 --> 00:31:31.380 line:84%
The entire premise
of Black Lives Matter

00:31:31.380 --> 00:31:34.840 line:84%
is antithetical to the
aspirations of equality

00:31:34.840 --> 00:31:37.170 line:90%
in King's hopeful sense.

00:31:37.170 --> 00:31:42.390 line:84%
Indeed, with its angry codicil
that asserting all lives matter

00:31:42.390 --> 00:31:45.840 line:84%
is racist, and
therefore impermissible.

00:31:45.840 --> 00:31:49.890 line:84%
The BLM movement is an
inversion of King's message,

00:31:49.890 --> 00:31:54.390 line:84%
just as it is an affront to
the ideal of impartiality

00:31:54.390 --> 00:31:57.810 line:84%
on which the rule
of law depends.

00:31:57.810 --> 00:32:02.520 line:84%
But the attack on that ideal
long predates the BLM movement.

00:32:02.520 --> 00:32:06.120 line:84%
It is woven deeply into the
culture of repudiation that

00:32:06.120 --> 00:32:09.120 line:84%
fueled the radicalism
of the 1960s

00:32:09.120 --> 00:32:11.310 line:84%
and undermined the
humanistic tradition

00:32:11.310 --> 00:32:15.360 line:84%
that, once upon a time,
our universities existed

00:32:15.360 --> 00:32:18.840 line:90%
to foster and hand down.

00:32:18.840 --> 00:32:21.750 line:84%
The cultural critic,
Christopher Lasch,

00:32:21.750 --> 00:32:24.660 line:90%
diagnosed it with great clarity.

00:32:24.660 --> 00:32:28.260 line:84%
"A misplaced
compassion," Lasch wrote

00:32:28.260 --> 00:32:32.190 line:84%
in his posthumously published
book The Revolt of the Elites,

00:32:32.190 --> 00:32:37.950 line:84%
"degrades both the victims, who
are reduced to objects of pity,

00:32:37.950 --> 00:32:40.950 line:84%
and their would be
benefactors, who find it easier

00:32:40.950 --> 00:32:44.370 line:84%
to pity their fellow
citizens than to hold them up

00:32:44.370 --> 00:32:47.790 line:84%
to impersonal standards,
attainment of which

00:32:47.790 --> 00:32:51.600 line:90%
would entitle them to respect."

00:32:51.600 --> 00:32:55.350 line:84%
Lasch, an old fashioned
liberal, understood

00:32:55.350 --> 00:32:59.100 line:84%
that embracing democracy
does not entail

00:32:59.100 --> 00:33:01.830 line:90%
a debasement of standards.

00:33:01.830 --> 00:33:05.310 line:84%
Equality, rightly
understood, requires

00:33:05.310 --> 00:33:11.220 line:84%
a respect for impartial
canons of achievement.

00:33:11.220 --> 00:33:14.820 line:84%
This insight lay at the center
of Matthew Arnold's Culture

00:33:14.820 --> 00:33:17.010 line:90%
and Anarchy.

00:33:17.010 --> 00:33:19.440 line:84%
"Culture," as
Arnold regarded it,

00:33:19.440 --> 00:33:25.290 line:84%
"does not try to teach down to
the level of inferior classes.

00:33:25.290 --> 00:33:28.380 line:84%
It seeks to do
away with classes,

00:33:28.380 --> 00:33:32.400 line:84%
to make the best that has been
thought and known in the world

00:33:32.400 --> 00:33:35.910 line:90%
current everywhere.

00:33:35.910 --> 00:33:39.660 line:84%
Hence, it is," Arnold wrote,
"that the men of culture

00:33:39.660 --> 00:33:43.110 line:84%
are the true
apostles of equality.

00:33:43.110 --> 00:33:45.030 line:84%
The great men of
culture are those

00:33:45.030 --> 00:33:47.370 line:84%
who have had a
passion for diffusing,

00:33:47.370 --> 00:33:52.080 line:84%
for making prevail, for
caring from one end of society

00:33:52.080 --> 00:33:58.340 line:84%
to the other the best knowledge,
the best ideas of their time."

00:33:58.340 --> 00:34:01.190 line:84%
Today, of course, the
contention that some ideas

00:34:01.190 --> 00:34:03.470 line:84%
are better than
others, let alone

00:34:03.470 --> 00:34:06.290 line:84%
that some deserve to
be called the best,

00:34:06.290 --> 00:34:11.639 line:84%
is rejected as an elitist
crime against diversity.

00:34:11.639 --> 00:34:14.590 line:84%
The cult or the
ideology of diversity,

00:34:14.590 --> 00:34:17.980 line:84%
what we have baptized as
identity politics, which

00:34:17.980 --> 00:34:21.940 line:84%
represents the systematic
rejection of everything

00:34:21.940 --> 00:34:26.980 line:84%
connected with impartiality and
objective impersonal standards,

00:34:26.980 --> 00:34:31.659 line:84%
is the enabling presupposition
of the destructive cauldron

00:34:31.659 --> 00:34:37.330 line:84%
of racialist obsession in which
our society is now marinating.

00:34:37.330 --> 00:34:40.780 line:84%
The irony is that our
current intolerance

00:34:40.780 --> 00:34:45.370 line:84%
is the perverted progeny of
the primary liberal value

00:34:45.370 --> 00:34:47.080 line:90%
of openness.

00:34:47.080 --> 00:34:50.590 line:84%
"It used to be," to quote
Allan Bloom one last time,

00:34:50.590 --> 00:34:52.750 line:84%
"that openness was
the virtue that

00:34:52.750 --> 00:34:57.190 line:84%
permitted us to seek the
good by using reason.

00:34:57.190 --> 00:35:00.100 line:84%
It now means
accepting everything

00:35:00.100 --> 00:35:02.350 line:90%
and denying reasons power.

00:35:02.350 --> 00:35:07.030 line:84%
The unrestrained and thoughtless
pursuit of openness,"

00:35:07.030 --> 00:35:11.920 line:84%
he concludes, "has rendered
openness meaningless."

00:35:11.920 --> 00:35:16.240 line:84%
If you doubt this, you could
tune in to the evening news

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:18.700 line:84%
and listen to fancy
people explain

00:35:18.700 --> 00:35:21.010 line:84%
why destroying someone
else's property

00:35:21.010 --> 00:35:25.180 line:84%
is not violence, while
keeping one's own counsel

00:35:25.180 --> 00:35:27.760 line:84%
and refusing to join
in the mob and ritual

00:35:27.760 --> 00:35:31.705 line:84%
origins of denunciation and
self-abasement is violence.

00:35:34.230 --> 00:35:37.830 line:84%
A couple of concluding
observations.

00:35:37.830 --> 00:35:42.390 line:84%
In his book Notes Towards the
Definition of Culture, T.S.

00:35:42.390 --> 00:35:46.410 line:84%
Eliot noted that, quote,
"culture is the one thing we

00:35:46.410 --> 00:35:48.960 line:90%
cannot deliberately aim at.

00:35:48.960 --> 00:35:53.220 line:84%
It is the product of a variety
of more or less harmonious

00:35:53.220 --> 00:35:59.840 line:84%
activities, each pursued
for its own sake."

00:35:59.840 --> 00:36:02.150 line:90%
For its own sake--

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:07.160 line:84%
that is one simple idea that
is everywhere in peril today.

00:36:07.160 --> 00:36:11.000 line:84%
When we plant a garden, it is
bootless to strive directly

00:36:11.000 --> 00:36:13.060 line:90%
for camellias.

00:36:13.060 --> 00:36:18.560 line:84%
They are the natural product
of our care, nurture, and time.

00:36:18.560 --> 00:36:22.040 line:84%
We can manage it, still,
when it comes to agriculture.

00:36:22.040 --> 00:36:25.550 line:84%
When we turn our hands
to cultura animi,

00:36:25.550 --> 00:36:27.860 line:84%
to the cultivation
of the soul, we

00:36:27.860 --> 00:36:32.900 line:84%
seem to be considerably
less successful.

00:36:32.900 --> 00:36:37.430 line:84%
Let me end by noting that
the opposite of conservatism

00:36:37.430 --> 00:36:44.010 line:84%
or conservative is not
liberal but ephemeral.

00:36:44.010 --> 00:36:47.670 line:84%
Russell Kirk once observed
that he was conservative

00:36:47.670 --> 00:36:52.290 line:84%
because he was liberal, that
is committed to freedom.

00:36:52.290 --> 00:36:56.010 line:84%
Kirk's formulation
may sound paradoxical,

00:36:56.010 --> 00:36:59.760 line:90%
but it touches on a great truth.

00:36:59.760 --> 00:37:03.090 line:84%
To be conservative,
that means wanting

00:37:03.090 --> 00:37:05.970 line:84%
to conserve what
is worth preserving

00:37:05.970 --> 00:37:12.270 line:84%
from the ravages of time and
ideology, evil and stupidity,

00:37:12.270 --> 00:37:15.450 line:90%
so that freedom may thrive.

00:37:15.450 --> 00:37:20.520 line:84%
In some plump eras, the task
is so easy we can almost

00:37:20.520 --> 00:37:23.930 line:90%
forget how necessary it is.

00:37:23.930 --> 00:37:26.930 line:84%
At other times, the
enemies of civilization

00:37:26.930 --> 00:37:31.010 line:84%
transform the task of
preserving our culture

00:37:31.010 --> 00:37:34.300 line:90%
into a battle for survival.

00:37:34.300 --> 00:37:38.090 line:84%
And that, I believe-- and
I say it regretfully--

00:37:38.090 --> 00:37:40.650 line:90%
is where we are today.

00:37:40.650 --> 00:37:41.150 line:90%
Thank you.

00:37:41.150 --> 00:37:44.601 line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

00:37:47.560 --> 00:37:53.730 line:84%
I'm happy to entertain comments,
questions, and [INAUDIBLE]..

00:37:53.730 --> 00:37:54.430 line:90%
SPEAKER 3: Yes.

00:37:54.430 --> 00:37:55.620 line:90%
Thank you, Mr. Kimball.

00:37:55.620 --> 00:37:58.780 line:84%
Mr. Kimball has agreed to
sign copies of his book,

00:37:58.780 --> 00:38:01.788 line:84%
Who Rules?, Sovereignty,
Nationalism,

00:38:01.788 --> 00:38:04.530 line:84%
and the Fate of Freedom
in the 21st Century,

00:38:04.530 --> 00:38:07.740 line:84%
following this session on
the second floor of Searle.

00:38:07.740 --> 00:38:09.413 line:84%
We now have some time
for a Q&amp;A, please

00:38:09.413 --> 00:38:11.580 line:84%
make your way down the aisle
if you have a question.

00:38:25.460 --> 00:38:28.130 line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I've noticed a lot
of schools similar to Hillsdale

00:38:28.130 --> 00:38:32.080 line:84%
have a very small student body,
while the schools that maybe

00:38:32.080 --> 00:38:34.880 line:84%
advocate for some of these
degenerative educational

00:38:34.880 --> 00:38:37.455 line:84%
practices have a really
high student body.

00:38:37.455 --> 00:38:40.280 line:84%
And I was wondering if
there's a way we can prevent

00:38:40.280 --> 00:38:42.620 line:84%
the masses from having
such a poor education

00:38:42.620 --> 00:38:48.822 line:84%
if schools like this are very,
very few and far between.

00:38:48.822 --> 00:38:51.280 line:84%
ROGER KIMBALL: I would say that
Hillsdale is-- can everyone

00:38:51.280 --> 00:38:51.760 line:90%
hear me?

00:38:51.760 --> 00:38:52.220 line:90%
Do I need to get back--

00:38:52.220 --> 00:38:53.375 line:84%
SPEAKER 4: There are
people watching online,

00:38:53.375 --> 00:38:54.890 line:90%
so you got to go get your mic.

00:38:54.890 --> 00:38:57.930 line:90%
ROGER KIMBALL: OK, great.

00:38:57.930 --> 00:39:01.230 line:84%
I would say that Hillsdale
is unique, not just unusual.

00:39:01.230 --> 00:39:07.200 line:84%
But unfortunately, it's worse
than your question suggests

00:39:07.200 --> 00:39:13.020 line:84%
because many of the
very worst Petri dishes

00:39:13.020 --> 00:39:17.880 line:84%
incubating this disease
are small schools.

00:39:17.880 --> 00:39:20.580 line:84%
All of the top--
all, I believe, all

00:39:20.580 --> 00:39:28.620 line:84%
of the so-called top liberal
arts, fancy schools, especially

00:39:28.620 --> 00:39:31.260 line:84%
in the East for
some reason, they

00:39:31.260 --> 00:39:33.780 line:84%
may have a student body
of a couple thousand,

00:39:33.780 --> 00:39:34.710 line:90%
or a few thousand.

00:39:34.710 --> 00:39:35.310 line:90%
They're small.

00:39:38.010 --> 00:39:39.690 line:90%
They're expensive.

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:43.060 line:90%
They have too much money.

00:39:43.060 --> 00:39:48.600 line:84%
But they are assiduous
in promulgating

00:39:48.600 --> 00:39:53.370 line:84%
all of the worst aspects
of this cultural revolution

00:39:53.370 --> 00:39:55.000 line:90%
that I intimated.

00:39:55.000 --> 00:39:57.030 line:84%
They're just as bad
as the huge schools.

00:39:57.030 --> 00:40:02.310 line:84%
And in fact, worse,
because the large schools,

00:40:02.310 --> 00:40:07.380 line:84%
while they may be intellectually
less distinguished,

00:40:07.380 --> 00:40:12.090 line:84%
are, in many cases, given
over to other pursuits.

00:40:12.090 --> 00:40:15.270 line:84%
They're not really concerned
with liberal arts education,

00:40:15.270 --> 00:40:18.600 line:84%
although they may say so when
they send out their fundraising

00:40:18.600 --> 00:40:19.880 line:90%
letters and so on.

00:40:19.880 --> 00:40:22.440 line:84%
But they're really concerned
about, in many cases--

00:40:22.440 --> 00:40:24.540 line:90%
not in all, but in many cases--

00:40:24.540 --> 00:40:28.307 line:84%
as a kind of vocational
education, which is fine.

00:40:28.307 --> 00:40:29.640 line:90%
There's nothing wrong with that.

00:40:29.640 --> 00:40:33.900 line:84%
But they're not really
part of the effort

00:40:33.900 --> 00:40:37.030 line:84%
to pass on this liberal
tradition I was talking about.

00:40:37.030 --> 00:40:41.270 line:84%
So I think it's not just
a matter of numbers,

00:40:41.270 --> 00:40:42.855 line:90%
it's really a matter of quality.

00:40:46.257 --> 00:40:48.090 line:84%
AUDIENCE: Thank you for
coming, Mr. Kimball.

00:40:48.090 --> 00:40:51.510 line:84%
I think I started reading
The New Criterion,

00:40:51.510 --> 00:40:53.980 line:84%
and my father would
earmark it for me, probably

00:40:53.980 --> 00:40:55.540 line:90%
by the time I was 14 or so.

00:40:55.540 --> 00:40:57.072 line:84%
And I don't know,
has anybody ever--

00:40:57.072 --> 00:40:58.030 line:90%
maybe it's the bow tie.

00:40:58.030 --> 00:40:59.620 line:84%
I think you bear
some resemblance

00:40:59.620 --> 00:41:02.065 line:84%
to Duncan Stroik,
the chapel architect.

00:41:02.065 --> 00:41:04.690 line:84%
I don't know, did
anybody else notice that?

00:41:04.690 --> 00:41:06.670 line:84%
ROGER KIMBALL:
Actually, he's my twin.

00:41:06.670 --> 00:41:08.080 line:90%
AUDIENCE: Makes sense.

00:41:08.080 --> 00:41:13.140 line:84%
I just wanted to ask,
you mentioned that for--

00:41:13.140 --> 00:41:15.430 line:84%
it's not the idea
that education ought

00:41:15.430 --> 00:41:18.880 line:84%
to be free from
ideological bent itself

00:41:18.880 --> 00:41:21.010 line:84%
and ideological
or philosophical,

00:41:21.010 --> 00:41:24.220 line:84%
if you will forgive the
equivocation, proposition

00:41:24.220 --> 00:41:26.500 line:84%
when considered in the
context of a modern state.

00:41:26.500 --> 00:41:28.690 line:84%
So I say the modern
state in contrast

00:41:28.690 --> 00:41:31.480 line:84%
to the ancient or
medieval regimes,

00:41:31.480 --> 00:41:34.600 line:84%
which did not have the power to
be so all-encompassing in terms

00:41:34.600 --> 00:41:39.590 line:84%
of state or government
involvement in education.

00:41:39.590 --> 00:41:42.520 line:84%
So for example, today,
Hillsdale's independence

00:41:42.520 --> 00:41:46.480 line:84%
from government seems to imply
an ideological bent, when

00:41:46.480 --> 00:41:48.540 line:90%
ideally it wouldn't, right?

00:41:54.230 --> 00:41:58.200 line:84%
ROGER KIMBALL: Well,
every society, and indeed

00:41:58.200 --> 00:42:02.550 line:84%
every common pursuit,
depends upon an affirmation

00:42:02.550 --> 00:42:07.160 line:90%
of certain values.

00:42:07.160 --> 00:42:12.810 line:84%
And there is a sense in which
that affirmation is political.

00:42:12.810 --> 00:42:17.310 line:84%
But when it comes to
liberal education,

00:42:17.310 --> 00:42:24.650 line:84%
the political affirmation
turns importantly on the idea

00:42:24.650 --> 00:42:27.500 line:84%
that none of us has a
monopoly on the truth.

00:42:27.500 --> 00:42:32.090 line:84%
And therefore, it
is a good thing

00:42:32.090 --> 00:42:35.990 line:84%
to foster a community
where people

00:42:35.990 --> 00:42:40.840 line:84%
can follow the
argument according

00:42:40.840 --> 00:42:44.620 line:84%
to their own best lights and
talk about it with other people

00:42:44.620 --> 00:42:45.280 line:90%
and debate it.

00:42:48.290 --> 00:42:51.770 line:84%
That is not a
political activity,

00:42:51.770 --> 00:42:57.140 line:84%
although it depends
upon a previous decision

00:42:57.140 --> 00:43:02.240 line:84%
that being able to engage
in that kind of free debate

00:43:02.240 --> 00:43:05.670 line:90%
is a worthwhile thing to do.

00:43:05.670 --> 00:43:11.780 line:84%
So when people say,
well, Hillsdale

00:43:11.780 --> 00:43:15.750 line:84%
is just as politicized as
Yale or Williams or something

00:43:15.750 --> 00:43:25.140 line:84%
because their decision to be
free from government intrusion

00:43:25.140 --> 00:43:28.710 line:90%
is a political act.

00:43:28.710 --> 00:43:31.200 line:90%
And there's some truth to that.

00:43:31.200 --> 00:43:33.630 line:84%
But where that
falls down, I think,

00:43:33.630 --> 00:43:43.410 line:84%
is in failing to recognize that
by essentially taking yourself

00:43:43.410 --> 00:43:47.370 line:84%
out of the influence
of people who

00:43:47.370 --> 00:43:53.160 line:84%
want to rule the way in
which you conduct yourselves,

00:43:53.160 --> 00:44:03.840 line:84%
you have not become slaves to
somebody else but are free.

00:44:03.840 --> 00:44:08.160 line:90%
So it seems to me that it's--

00:44:08.160 --> 00:44:11.400 line:84%
you often see people make
this rhetorical move.

00:44:11.400 --> 00:44:14.970 line:84%
They'll say, well, isn't
that just as political

00:44:14.970 --> 00:44:18.000 line:90%
as being non-political?

00:44:18.000 --> 00:44:21.320 line:84%
And you saw this, for
example, in the phrase--

00:44:21.320 --> 00:44:24.000 line:84%
I alluded to it, although
I don't think I quoted it--

00:44:24.000 --> 00:44:29.520 line:84%
this summer people would
say silence is violence.

00:44:29.520 --> 00:44:33.540 line:84%
But no it isn't,
violence is violence.

00:44:33.540 --> 00:44:35.800 line:90%
Silence is silence.

00:44:35.800 --> 00:44:43.290 line:84%
And the metabolism
of this phenomenon

00:44:43.290 --> 00:44:45.030 line:84%
that you're talking
about is something

00:44:45.030 --> 00:44:49.740 line:84%
that it's very important
to be alert to because it's

00:44:49.740 --> 00:44:54.870 line:84%
used not in good faith, but
it's used as a sort of bludgeon

00:44:54.870 --> 00:45:02.910 line:84%
to try to make it impossible to
pursue a genuinely liberal arts

00:45:02.910 --> 00:45:05.730 line:84%
education, and beyond
that, to pursue

00:45:05.730 --> 00:45:10.440 line:84%
a genuinely liberal society,
where people live together

00:45:10.440 --> 00:45:14.520 line:84%
because they affirm certain
basic values about the nature

00:45:14.520 --> 00:45:18.330 line:84%
of their society but are
otherwise free to pursue

00:45:18.330 --> 00:45:25.650 line:84%
their lives and to
disagree and engage

00:45:25.650 --> 00:45:27.720 line:90%
in public, spirited debate.

00:45:27.720 --> 00:45:31.860 line:84%
We are in a situation now, not
just in our educational lives

00:45:31.860 --> 00:45:38.190 line:84%
but in the life of this country,
where dissent is regarded

00:45:38.190 --> 00:45:40.560 line:90%
as a kind of criminal act.

00:45:40.560 --> 00:45:45.240 line:84%
And it's instead of--
it's sort of like heresy.

00:45:45.240 --> 00:45:52.500 line:84%
And down that path is a
very, very dangerous end.

00:45:52.500 --> 00:45:57.390 line:84%
I think we still have time
to step back from the abyss,

00:45:57.390 --> 00:45:59.010 line:90%
but we are close to the abyss.

00:46:03.270 --> 00:46:05.130 line:90%
AUDIENCE: Thanks for your talk.

00:46:05.130 --> 00:46:07.390 line:90%
So there were two sides here.

00:46:07.390 --> 00:46:09.840 line:84%
We have diversity
and multiculturalism,

00:46:09.840 --> 00:46:13.020 line:84%
which I don't think are
necessarily inherently bad,

00:46:13.020 --> 00:46:15.210 line:84%
but they do have to be
balanced with preserving

00:46:15.210 --> 00:46:19.185 line:84%
a homogeneous national culture
necessary to sustain a country.

00:46:19.185 --> 00:46:20.310 line:90%
So there's a tension there.

00:46:20.310 --> 00:46:21.900 line:84%
How much nuance
would you introduce

00:46:21.900 --> 00:46:23.040 line:90%
into that conversation?

00:46:30.250 --> 00:46:32.320 line:84%
ROGER KIMBALL: If I
understood your question

00:46:32.320 --> 00:46:35.590 line:84%
it's, multiculturalism
is not necessarily bad

00:46:35.590 --> 00:46:39.370 line:84%
because it provides
diversity and that sort of--

00:46:39.370 --> 00:46:40.960 line:84%
well, the problem
is that what goes

00:46:40.960 --> 00:46:44.560 line:84%
under the name of
multiculturalism

00:46:44.560 --> 00:46:47.800 line:90%
is there's no multi about it.

00:46:47.800 --> 00:46:50.150 line:84%
It really is a kind
of, as I said--

00:46:50.150 --> 00:46:56.450 line:84%
I think I've said-- of
a monocultural passion.

00:46:56.450 --> 00:47:01.530 line:84%
So if you actually
look at what is

00:47:01.530 --> 00:47:05.490 line:90%
being taught as multicultural.

00:47:05.490 --> 00:47:09.120 line:84%
You'll find that really it's
just a series of cliches.

00:47:09.120 --> 00:47:12.150 line:84%
The people who are promulgating
it don't know, for example,

00:47:12.150 --> 00:47:13.262 line:90%
other languages.

00:47:13.262 --> 00:47:14.970 line:84%
If you really wanted
to be multicultural,

00:47:14.970 --> 00:47:18.000 line:84%
you would learn the languages
of these other cultures

00:47:18.000 --> 00:47:21.060 line:84%
that you were pretending
to talk about.

00:47:21.060 --> 00:47:22.350 line:90%
But no, it's really--

00:47:22.350 --> 00:47:28.650 line:84%
it's a kind of fancy,
polysyllabic form

00:47:28.650 --> 00:47:37.020 line:84%
of monocultural
passion, at least

00:47:37.020 --> 00:47:39.630 line:84%
that's how I have
experienced it whenever I've

00:47:39.630 --> 00:47:42.570 line:84%
looked into the way
in which it's actually

00:47:42.570 --> 00:47:44.480 line:90%
promulgated on campuses.

00:47:44.480 --> 00:47:51.510 line:84%
It's taught not as a way of
learning about other cultures,

00:47:51.510 --> 00:47:56.110 line:84%
but as a way of undermining
Western culture.

00:47:56.110 --> 00:48:00.300 line:84%
It's all cultures are
equal, except they're all

00:48:00.300 --> 00:48:02.250 line:90%
superior to the West.

00:48:02.250 --> 00:48:08.730 line:84%
It's a version of what Orwell
talked about in Animal Farm.

00:48:08.730 --> 00:48:11.310 line:84%
All cultures are--
all animals are equal,

00:48:11.310 --> 00:48:12.920 line:84%
but some are more
equal than others.

00:48:18.230 --> 00:48:20.080 line:84%
SPEAKER 3: Question
to the speaker's left.

00:48:20.080 --> 00:48:21.230 line:90%
AUDIENCE: Hi.

00:48:21.230 --> 00:48:23.660 line:84%
Thank you for being
here, Mr. Kimball.

00:48:23.660 --> 00:48:25.850 line:84%
This speaks to the question
that was just asked.

00:48:25.850 --> 00:48:28.190 line:84%
But in your speech
you discussed how

00:48:28.190 --> 00:48:30.680 line:84%
multiculturalism
and pluralism have

00:48:30.680 --> 00:48:33.220 line:84%
corrosive effects
on our education

00:48:33.220 --> 00:48:34.640 line:90%
and our society at large.

00:48:34.640 --> 00:48:40.280 line:84%
But I was wondering, is there
a seed of good or something

00:48:40.280 --> 00:48:42.480 line:90%
valuable in these ideas?

00:48:42.480 --> 00:48:46.670 line:84%
And in other words, what
does the non-Western world

00:48:46.670 --> 00:48:47.600 line:90%
have to teach us?

00:48:51.270 --> 00:48:53.020 line:84%
ROGER KIMBALL: Well,
the non-Western world

00:48:53.020 --> 00:48:54.430 line:90%
might have a lot to teach us.

00:48:54.430 --> 00:48:58.330 line:84%
But it's been my
experience, first

00:48:58.330 --> 00:49:03.720 line:84%
of all, that what goes under
the banner of multiculturalism

00:49:03.720 --> 00:49:06.240 line:84%
actually doesn't
really learn very much

00:49:06.240 --> 00:49:08.730 line:90%
about the non-Western world.

00:49:08.730 --> 00:49:16.210 line:84%
And secondly, it seems to
me that, as Americans, we

00:49:16.210 --> 00:49:25.984 line:84%
want to be able to affirm who we
are first before pretending to,

00:49:25.984 --> 00:49:33.050 line:84%
A, criticize our society
and, B, adopt as if we are

00:49:33.050 --> 00:49:35.915 line:90%
a vacationing other cultures.

00:49:38.470 --> 00:49:40.480 line:84%
I think if you
actually look at what

00:49:40.480 --> 00:49:47.140 line:84%
is taught in colleges under
the banner of multiculturalism,

00:49:47.140 --> 00:49:52.660 line:84%
you'll find that most often
it's composed very largely

00:49:52.660 --> 00:49:55.180 line:84%
of anti-Western
animus rather than

00:49:55.180 --> 00:49:58.000 line:84%
genuine intellectual curiosity
about other cultures.

00:50:01.030 --> 00:50:07.250 line:84%
That said, I think it's
important to understand that--

00:50:07.250 --> 00:50:09.650 line:84%
I think I mentioned
this just in passing--

00:50:09.650 --> 00:50:13.220 line:84%
that all cultures
really are not equal.

00:50:13.220 --> 00:50:17.840 line:84%
And Saul Bellow got
into a lot of trouble

00:50:17.840 --> 00:50:22.520 line:84%
when he asked, where's
the Tolstoy of the Zulus?

00:50:22.520 --> 00:50:24.080 line:90%
People didn't like that.

00:50:24.080 --> 00:50:27.170 line:84%
But that is not an illegitimate
question, it seems to me.

00:50:27.170 --> 00:50:28.250 line:90%
Now there might be.

00:50:28.250 --> 00:50:31.910 line:84%
There might be a great
novelist of the Zulus.

00:50:31.910 --> 00:50:33.740 line:90%
I don't know.

00:50:33.740 --> 00:50:38.480 line:84%
But it's important to be able
to tell the truth about things

00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:41.150 line:84%
and to call things
by their real names.

00:50:41.150 --> 00:50:46.490 line:84%
And I think that there's
a great sentimentality

00:50:46.490 --> 00:50:50.840 line:84%
among some Americans about
the virtues of other cultures

00:50:50.840 --> 00:50:55.340 line:84%
that the more you look at them,
the less justified that seems.

00:50:55.340 --> 00:51:00.500 line:84%
A novelist who was very
brilliant, I think,

00:51:00.500 --> 00:51:06.380 line:84%
and brutal about exposing
that was V.S. Naipaul,

00:51:06.380 --> 00:51:11.750 line:84%
who, himself, was from Trinidad,
I think, and really understood

00:51:11.750 --> 00:51:15.110 line:84%
a lot about other
cultures, but he also

00:51:15.110 --> 00:51:21.260 line:84%
understood the toxic
effects of sentimentalizing

00:51:21.260 --> 00:51:24.830 line:84%
those cultures, especially
for people from the West.

00:51:28.480 --> 00:51:31.150 line:84%
AUDIENCE: Thank you
for coming on tonight.

00:51:31.150 --> 00:51:32.890 line:84%
This relates to an
earlier question,

00:51:32.890 --> 00:51:35.380 line:84%
so I apologize for
repeating anything here.

00:51:35.380 --> 00:51:36.940 line:84%
But one thing you
mentioned was how

00:51:36.940 --> 00:51:41.640 line:84%
the apolitical has
become politicized

00:51:41.640 --> 00:51:45.277 line:84%
and the intellectual,
political is entering into it.

00:51:45.277 --> 00:51:47.860 line:84%
And I was just wondering if you
could speak a little more what

00:51:47.860 --> 00:51:49.010 line:90%
do we do about this.

00:51:49.010 --> 00:51:53.470 line:84%
So for example, a book like
Huckleberry Finn, which

00:51:53.470 --> 00:51:57.670 line:84%
could be put in a
great books curriculum,

00:51:57.670 --> 00:52:00.400 line:84%
it's now become a
political statement

00:52:00.400 --> 00:52:02.740 line:84%
to put it in the
curriculum in a way

00:52:02.740 --> 00:52:05.770 line:90%
that it wasn't not too long ago.

00:52:05.770 --> 00:52:09.490 line:84%
So is there a way we have to
depoliticize the apolitical,

00:52:09.490 --> 00:52:13.150 line:90%
or is it too late for that?

00:52:13.150 --> 00:52:16.720 line:84%
What can we do to recover just
reading Huck Finn because it's

00:52:16.720 --> 00:52:20.890 line:84%
a great book, and for no other
political aim other than that.

00:52:23.745 --> 00:52:26.120 line:84%
ROGER KIMBALL: Yeah, I think
that's a very good question.

00:52:31.530 --> 00:52:37.850 line:84%
What we can do is say, quite
frankly, to the people who say,

00:52:37.850 --> 00:52:45.710 line:84%
we're offended by Huckleberry
Finn, or "Othello,"

00:52:45.710 --> 00:52:51.290 line:84%
or teaching Chaucer, who's,
after all, a dead white guy--

00:52:51.290 --> 00:52:54.890 line:84%
some famous college
has just taken

00:52:54.890 --> 00:52:58.130 line:90%
Chaucer out of its curriculum--

00:52:58.130 --> 00:53:00.440 line:90%
is to say no.

00:53:00.440 --> 00:53:03.360 line:90%
We are going to--

00:53:03.360 --> 00:53:07.610 line:84%
the fact that you're
offended offends me.

00:53:07.610 --> 00:53:15.650 line:84%
And so we're not going to
submit to this politically

00:53:15.650 --> 00:53:19.100 line:90%
correct effort at intimidation.

00:53:19.100 --> 00:53:26.090 line:84%
Instead, we are going
to look to the past

00:53:26.090 --> 00:53:32.990 line:84%
for the criterion of literary,
artistic, cultural achievement.

00:53:32.990 --> 00:53:34.400 line:90%
Why?

00:53:34.400 --> 00:53:39.080 line:84%
Well, David Hume said,
what is a work of genius?

00:53:39.080 --> 00:53:41.960 line:84%
And he said, it's
durable appreciation,

00:53:41.960 --> 00:53:46.920 line:84%
those works that have been
appreciated for a long time.

00:53:46.920 --> 00:53:51.960 line:84%
And of course, now that
phenomenon changes.

00:53:51.960 --> 00:53:56.040 line:90%
Things go in and out of fashion.

00:53:56.040 --> 00:54:00.790 line:84%
In the middle of
the 19th century,

00:54:00.790 --> 00:54:04.260 line:84%
people like Shelley and Byron
thought Tasso was terrific.

00:54:04.260 --> 00:54:07.560 line:84%
Very few people
read Tasso anymore.

00:54:07.560 --> 00:54:09.990 line:84%
There was a time
when Walter Scott

00:54:09.990 --> 00:54:14.220 line:84%
was thought to be top dog
when it came to novelists.

00:54:14.220 --> 00:54:17.620 line:84%
I think Scott is not really
read that much anymore.

00:54:17.620 --> 00:54:22.860 line:84%
But the point is, these
matters of taste, I think,

00:54:22.860 --> 00:54:27.840 line:84%
should be determined
by impartial standards.

00:54:27.840 --> 00:54:31.770 line:84%
I mean, taste is an
interesting thing, isn't it?

00:54:35.470 --> 00:54:38.200 line:84%
It's pretty clear, Immanuel
Kant pointed this out

00:54:38.200 --> 00:54:40.810 line:90%
in his Critique of Judgment.

00:54:40.810 --> 00:54:42.300 line:84%
There's different
kinds of taste.

00:54:42.300 --> 00:54:45.880 line:84%
You might like your steak well
done, I might like mine rare.

00:54:45.880 --> 00:54:48.370 line:84%
That's a kind of
aesthetic judgment,

00:54:48.370 --> 00:54:52.270 line:84%
and aesthetic meaning
having to do with sensation.

00:54:52.270 --> 00:54:55.660 line:84%
But we understand that
this sort of taste

00:54:55.660 --> 00:54:58.540 line:84%
that's involved in
literary judgment

00:54:58.540 --> 00:55:00.370 line:90%
is something different.

00:55:00.370 --> 00:55:01.970 line:84%
And what is
different about that?

00:55:01.970 --> 00:55:04.180 line:84%
Well, Kant has
his little schema.

00:55:04.180 --> 00:55:06.580 line:84%
Actually, it's not
so little, but he

00:55:06.580 --> 00:55:08.140 line:90%
has a way of explaining that.

00:55:08.140 --> 00:55:14.470 line:84%
But it's to basically appeal
to a larger community.

00:55:14.470 --> 00:55:20.710 line:84%
And he says that aesthetic
judgment, in that sense,

00:55:20.710 --> 00:55:26.980 line:84%
has an ennobling and
enlarging effect.

00:55:26.980 --> 00:55:32.620 line:84%
Well, that's obviously a
hint of a long discussion.

00:55:32.620 --> 00:55:34.760 line:90%
Why is it, you might ask?

00:55:34.760 --> 00:55:38.740 line:84%
Why do we think that works
of art are important?

00:55:38.740 --> 00:55:42.940 line:84%
Clearly we do because we
spend millions and millions

00:55:42.940 --> 00:55:45.970 line:84%
and millions of dollars
building museums and acquiring

00:55:45.970 --> 00:55:46.570 line:90%
works of art.

00:55:46.570 --> 00:55:48.720 line:84%
Why do we think
education is important?

00:55:48.720 --> 00:55:50.980 line:90%
Again, clearly we do.

00:55:50.980 --> 00:55:54.320 line:84%
I mean, there are 4,500
institutions of education--

00:55:54.320 --> 00:55:56.230 line:84%
higher education
in this country.

00:55:56.230 --> 00:55:58.660 line:84%
So we think that there's
something important about it,

00:55:58.660 --> 00:56:00.760 line:84%
even if, to some
very large extent,

00:56:00.760 --> 00:56:03.050 line:90%
we've lost sight of that.

00:56:03.050 --> 00:56:06.670 line:84%
But it seems to me that
the most important loss

00:56:06.670 --> 00:56:08.560 line:84%
is exactly what
you're talking about,

00:56:08.560 --> 00:56:13.600 line:84%
being able to judge these
works on their own terms.

00:56:13.600 --> 00:56:17.680 line:84%
That's why I mentioned
toward the end

00:56:17.680 --> 00:56:20.080 line:84%
that you do something
for its own sake.

00:56:20.080 --> 00:56:24.670 line:84%
It's not for some ulterior
utilitarian motive.

00:56:24.670 --> 00:56:27.670 line:84%
It's because it's
a good work of art.

00:56:27.670 --> 00:56:33.370 line:84%
And again, there's always going
to be discussion, disagreement

00:56:33.370 --> 00:56:34.120 line:90%
about that.

00:56:34.120 --> 00:56:36.210 line:84%
But that's what makes
life interesting,

00:56:36.210 --> 00:56:37.932 line:90%
that kind of disagreement.

00:56:37.932 --> 00:56:40.390 line:84%
To have someone say, no, you
can't read this because it has

00:56:40.390 --> 00:56:42.460 line:90%
words I don't approve of in it.

00:56:42.460 --> 00:56:46.395 line:90%
That's not on, I would say.

00:56:46.395 --> 00:56:47.770 line:84%
SPEAKER 3: Thank
you, Mr Kimball.

00:56:47.770 --> 00:56:50.830 line:84%
We have time for
one final question.

00:56:50.830 --> 00:56:53.920 line:84%
AUDIENCE: Hello, thank you
for your comments tonight.

00:56:53.920 --> 00:56:58.760 line:84%
My question is related to the
religious language you used,

00:56:58.760 --> 00:57:03.460 line:84%
specifically with some of the
opponents of liberal education.

00:57:03.460 --> 00:57:06.550 line:84%
And with that
level of commitment

00:57:06.550 --> 00:57:10.840 line:84%
to their ideas and
devaluation of reason

00:57:10.840 --> 00:57:13.540 line:84%
in dialogue, what do you see,
or what do you prescribe,

00:57:13.540 --> 00:57:15.940 line:84%
as a means to combat
that for people

00:57:15.940 --> 00:57:18.610 line:84%
that would adhere to reason
as being the superior

00:57:18.610 --> 00:57:22.910 line:90%
means to go about these issues?

00:57:22.910 --> 00:57:24.910 line:84%
ROGER KIMBALL: Let's make
sure I understand you.

00:57:28.400 --> 00:57:31.738 line:84%
Are you opposing reason
to religion, or--

00:57:31.738 --> 00:57:32.280 line:90%
AUDIENCE: No.

00:57:32.280 --> 00:57:38.070 line:84%
So when you were speaking of
some of the contemporary ideas,

00:57:38.070 --> 00:57:41.830 line:84%
you used language like
baptized and things like that.

00:57:41.830 --> 00:57:46.380 line:84%
And I think rightly, there is
a religious element of this.

00:57:46.380 --> 00:57:49.290 line:84%
But in that, they've
devalued reason

00:57:49.290 --> 00:57:51.780 line:84%
in a way that makes
it hard for people

00:57:51.780 --> 00:57:54.240 line:84%
that are liberals
in another sense

00:57:54.240 --> 00:57:56.100 line:90%
to use reason to combat it.

00:57:56.100 --> 00:57:58.740 line:84%
So what would you
prescribe for a means

00:57:58.740 --> 00:58:01.380 line:84%
to combat something that
is religious in nature

00:58:01.380 --> 00:58:04.290 line:84%
and antithetical
to using reason?

00:58:04.290 --> 00:58:08.320 line:90%
ROGER KIMBALL: OK, so the cult?

00:58:08.320 --> 00:58:11.160 line:84%
Yeah, well, I
think, first of all,

00:58:11.160 --> 00:58:15.030 line:84%
the first step is
recognizing what something is

00:58:15.030 --> 00:58:17.430 line:84%
and calling it by its
real name, as I just

00:58:17.430 --> 00:58:20.910 line:90%
said to the last chap.

00:58:20.910 --> 00:58:27.420 line:84%
There's a book by a man
named Norman Cohn called

00:58:27.420 --> 00:58:29.490 line:90%
the Pursuit of the Millennium.

00:58:29.490 --> 00:58:37.470 line:84%
And it's all about these
medieval movements,

00:58:37.470 --> 00:58:42.450 line:84%
the brotherhood of the
free spirit, people

00:58:42.450 --> 00:58:46.770 line:84%
who felt that they
had somehow achieved

00:58:46.770 --> 00:58:50.730 line:84%
a higher level of virtue
than the other people

00:58:50.730 --> 00:58:51.450 line:90%
in their society.

00:58:51.450 --> 00:58:54.980 line:84%
And this enabled them to
do all kinds of things.

00:58:54.980 --> 00:58:58.530 line:84%
They were not bound anymore
by the normal morality

00:58:58.530 --> 00:59:00.215 line:84%
of the people who
were not enlightened.

00:59:00.215 --> 00:59:01.590 line:84%
They could do
whatever they want,

00:59:01.590 --> 00:59:05.820 line:84%
because they occupied a
higher plane of enlightenment

00:59:05.820 --> 00:59:09.870 line:84%
and a direct connection, so
they thought, with the Godhead.

00:59:09.870 --> 00:59:12.090 line:84%
They might say
that all material--

00:59:12.090 --> 00:59:15.030 line:84%
the whole material
world is evil,

00:59:15.030 --> 00:59:18.900 line:84%
except, since I understand
that, I'm no longer bound

00:59:18.900 --> 00:59:21.320 line:90%
by the normal codes of morality.

00:59:21.320 --> 00:59:24.948 line:84%
If that sounds a lot like
what happened in the 1960s,

00:59:24.948 --> 00:59:25.740 line:90%
you're quite right.

00:59:25.740 --> 00:59:27.810 line:84%
That's also what
happened, by the way,

00:59:27.810 --> 00:59:28.950 line:90%
in the French Revolution.

00:59:28.950 --> 00:59:30.120 line:90%
Why was it?

00:59:30.120 --> 00:59:36.160 line:84%
Why was it that Robespierre,
the disciple of Jean-Jacques

00:59:36.160 --> 00:59:39.720 line:84%
Rousseau, why was it that
the word virtue was always

00:59:39.720 --> 00:59:40.410 line:90%
on his lips?

00:59:40.410 --> 00:59:43.830 line:84%
He took that word and that
concept from Rousseau,

00:59:43.830 --> 00:59:46.890 line:84%
and the idea being
that he wanted

00:59:46.890 --> 00:59:50.610 line:90%
to create a virtuous society.

00:59:50.610 --> 00:59:53.232 line:84%
Society was not virtuous,
Robespierre thought,

00:59:53.232 --> 00:59:54.940 line:84%
so we have to get rid
of certain people--

00:59:54.940 --> 00:59:59.070 line:84%
the people who are virtuous
in my sense, said Robespierre.

00:59:59.070 --> 01:00:00.630 line:90%
They have to be gotten rid of.

01:00:00.630 --> 01:00:06.300 line:84%
So the index of virtue
for the French Revolution

01:00:06.300 --> 01:00:08.820 line:84%
was the rapidity with which
the guillotine got rid

01:00:08.820 --> 01:00:11.040 line:90%
of those who were not virtuous.

01:00:11.040 --> 01:00:17.220 line:90%
It's a poisonous idea.

01:00:17.220 --> 01:00:21.720 line:84%
And again, you don't have to
look very far in our society

01:00:21.720 --> 01:00:27.450 line:84%
to see that kind of
sentiment at work.

01:00:27.450 --> 01:00:32.070 line:84%
If you pay attention to the
rhetoric of Black Lives Matter,

01:00:32.070 --> 01:00:37.860 line:84%
for example, or Antifa,
or certain members

01:00:37.860 --> 01:00:39.690 line:84%
of that large
building in Washington

01:00:39.690 --> 01:00:45.120 line:84%
that's now surrounded by a
12 foot fence and razor wire,

01:00:45.120 --> 01:00:50.700 line:84%
you hear scary echoes
of that kind of thing.

01:00:50.700 --> 01:00:53.460 line:84%
They're virtuous,
and you're not.

01:00:53.460 --> 01:00:56.610 line:84%
And they believe that
with a religious fervor

01:00:56.610 --> 01:01:01.560 line:84%
which does not invite
conversation or disagreement.

01:01:01.560 --> 01:01:07.500 line:84%
But like any heresy,
it invites condemnation

01:01:07.500 --> 01:01:11.200 line:90%
and being stamped out.

01:01:11.200 --> 01:01:11.740 line:90%
Thanks.

01:01:11.740 --> 01:01:13.870 line:84%
SPEAKER 3: Thank
you, Mr. Kimball.

